<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=catapult">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Catapult</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as catapults</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A catapult is a siege engine that hurls projectiles using tension, torsion or human traction, used before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> artillery. Chinese armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> used traction catapults extensively in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-warfare">siege warfare</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-xiangyang">siege of Xiangyang</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>'s forces deployed Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counterweight-trebuchet">counterweight trebuchets</a> that outranged the Chinese catapults.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/catapult.jpg" alt="Catapult" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Replica_catapult.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word catapult comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> catapulta, from the Greek katapeltēs, formed from kata, downwards, and pallō, to toss or hurl. A catapult launches a projectile at a great distance without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>, by the sudden release of stored energy: tension or torsion built up slowly in springs, bows, twisted rope or elastic. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzziah">Uzziah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> is recorded as equipping the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> with machines that shot great stones, and Jaina texts record that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru">Ajatashatru</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> used catapults against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavis</a> in the 5th century BCE. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> the mangonel appeared in the same century.</p><p>Greek catapults were invented in the early 4th century BCE. Diodorus Siculus describes a mechanical arrow-firing catapult devised by a Greek task force in 399 BCE and used at the siege of Motya in 397 BCE. It developed from the gastraphetes, or belly-bow, a foot-held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">crossbow</a> that stored more energy than ordinary bows.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>An Athenian arsenal inscription dated between 338 and 326 BCE is the first clear evidence for torsion catapults, which were more powerful than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">crossbow</a> types and came to dominate Greek and Roman artillery. The change was probably spurred by the engineers of Philip II of Macedonia, who used arrow-firing machines at the siege of Perinth in 340 BCE. Alexander the Great used catapults at the Battle of Jaxartes and in sieges. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> began to use them in their wars against Syracuse, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolia">Aetolia</a>, and later fitted ballistae to warships.</p><p>Medieval armies used ballistae, springalds, mangonels and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebuchet">trebuchets</a> against castles and walled cities, throwing stones, incendiary missiles, and sometimes carcasses or garbage over the walls. The trebuchet was probably the most powerful, in a traction form worked by people and a counterpoise form worked by a counterweight. At the siege of Stirling Castle in 1304 the army of Edward I built a giant trebuchet called Warwolf, which levelled a section of wall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Defences improved until catapults were largely ineffective, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 885–6 used nearly every siege instrument known to the classical world to little effect. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a> designed a catapult powered by a leaf spring and winding drum, which enthusiasts have since built. The last large-scale army use was in the trench warfare of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, when catapults threw hand grenades into enemy trenches until small mortars replaced them. Catapults also launched aircraft from ships, from British experiments in 1917 to common fittings on major warships by the 1930s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Catapult” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They held out for five years and fell only after Kublai brought in engineers from the Ilkhanate whose counterweight trebuchets outranged the defenders' <b>catapults</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The siege of Xiangyang and Fancheng (1268–1273) ended only after five years, when Persian engineers sent from the Ilkhanate built counterweight trebuchets, heavy <b>catapults</b> able to batter the walls with heavy stones; it shows the persistence, logistical skill and technological adaptability of Mongol operations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 397 BCE Dionysius I of Syracuse besieged Motya, building a mole across the lagoon and using siege towers and <b>catapults</b>; the city fell after fierce street fighting.</blockquote><a href="/state/motya">Motya</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>5th century BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Magadha, India</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Syracuse, Sicily (Greek) (399 BCE)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The Bible credits King Uzziah of Judah with stone-throwing machines about 750 BCE, but in a much later text.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">Crossbow<span>c. 650 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=catapult">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
